WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.700
The first film used Grb2 to route a receptor signal toward Ras.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.200
But what if two pathways share the same kinase?

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.640
Follow the same colors as one orange kinase faces two possible purple outputs.

4
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:21.593
Yeast mating uses a kinase route ending in Fus3.

5
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:25.953
Some upstream kinases also serve other responses.

6
00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:32.393
A pheromone input must reach the mating output without making the cell follow every

7
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:33.713
route that shares a part.

8
00:00:34.717 --> 00:00:39.337
A shared kinase does not carry a label saying which response should follow.

9
00:00:40.637 --> 00:00:43.257
Its meaning depends on the partners around it.

10
00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:48.897
The cell therefore needs an organizer that assembles the mating route under the

11
00:00:48.897 --> 00:00:49.897
mating input.

12
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:54.260
Ste5 is a green scaffold with no enzyme activity.

13
00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:01.620
Different regions bind Ste11, Ste7 and Fus3, so one Ste5 can hold all three.

14
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:06.000
This assembly can make productive encounters more likely.

15
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:14.063
Within the assembled route, Ste11 activates Ste7, and Ste7 activates Fus3.

16
00:01:14.063 --> 00:01:16.853
Fus3 helps prepare the cell for mating.

17
00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:20.873
The scaffold does not perform those kinase reactions.

18
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:24.193
It organizes the proteins that do.

19
00:01:25.217 --> 00:01:27.697
Proximity is only the first model.

20
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:32.517
Ste5 responds to pheromone-linked recruitment at the membrane.

21
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:35.117
Its shape opens there.

22
00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:42.317
A Ste5 domain also helps Ste7 activate Fus3, which is otherwise a poor substrate.

23
00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:46.010
The scaffold therefore helps select an output.

24
00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.570
It controls partners, place and activation, rather than locking every shared kinase

25
00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:58.510
away. The same shared parts can behave differently inside a different organized

26
00:01:58.510 --> 00:01:59.510
context.

27
00:02:00.333 --> 00:02:05.573
The filament-growth route uses Ste11 and Ste7 but ends at Kss1.

28
00:02:06.853 --> 00:02:12.573
One salt-stress branch uses Ste11, then a different middle kinase, and ends at Hog1.

29
00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:18.593
Only the mating route has Ste5, so only the mating route switches on Fus3.

30
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:23.027
Suppose the cell keeps adding empty Ste5 scaffolds.

31
00:02:24.367 --> 00:02:27.407
Must the number of complete kinase assemblies keep rising?

32
00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:31.807
Watch where each kinase might land, then make your prediction.

33
00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:44.590
No. In the simplest model, all three kinases must share one scaffold to signal.

34
00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.630
With scaffold in excess, they spread across different scaffold molecules.

35
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:54.150
Fewer scaffolds are complete, so predicted output falls.

36
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:58.530
This model effect is called combinatorial inhibition.

37
00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:01.870
Experiments support both layers.

38
00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:06.410
Ste5 binds the mating kinases on different regions.

39
00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:13.290
Later work showed that a Ste5 domain makes Fus3 easier for Ste7 to activate.

40
00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.290
Assembly and selective activation work together.

41
00:03:18.383 --> 00:03:19.703
Return to the fork.

42
00:03:21.023 --> 00:03:23.463
A shared kinase does not choose an output alone.

43
00:03:24.743 --> 00:03:29.823
The organizer around it changes which partners meet, where the complex forms and

44
00:03:29.823 --> 00:03:31.383
whether the last step can proceed.
